## 8. Gate calibration defense

- objective alignment: finite tests measure handover hygiene and admission control. They cannot validate product correctness or served model identity.
- risk calibration: actual fail-closed controls have positive and negative cases; exact role/model/provider and physical log containment remain material boundaries.
- negative/adversarial: original prefix, regex, committed forged receipt, late HEAD query and direct receipt probes plus cross-role, provider, junction and mixed checker error cases. Fixtures reside in private external clones; all workers stubbed.
- regression baseline: current frozen assertions reproduce 38/0, 34/0, 14/0. AO8 counts were 34/0 and 32/0 on a different subject; new assertions explain count growth. Counts alone are not gate coverage.
- anti-overfitting: qualify-only bypasses child protection; checking a parent path misses a junction at the log directory; isolated single-category failover tests miss mixed/irrelevant text. These are independent unseen anti-cases.
- judge calibration: deterministic commands take precedence; no LLM worker judge or real model was launched.
- HITL/domain calibration: no production/domain acceptance requested or inferred; existing public-release HOLD applies.
- evaluator integrity: repair-diff.txt records gate and fixture changes against the historical AO8-end commit. Gate/fixture edits are candidate changes, not self-validating authority; current authority remains frozen owner/assignment predicates. No separate authenticated evaluator promotion receipt was located, so none is claimed.

## 9. Verdict

`FAIL_CHALLENGE`

Both requested gates have confirmed blocking contradictions: S4-STAGE-B-HANDOVER-CLOSURE fails physical log containment and outer-checker artifact lifecycle; S4-MODEL-ROUTING-CONFORMANCE fails qualify-only authorization, role exception scope, provider binding and forbidden checker failover. Repairs to the finite old prefix, receipt, late-HEAD and default direct-qualification mechanisms are supported. Current aggregate manifest/distribution acceptance remains unresolved. This verdict is bound to the frozen commit and finite declared probes, not unreviewed concurrent changes.

## 10. Claim ceiling

- local/package: deterministic evidence establishes finite harness control flow only. Snapshot tar is identified; historical mount/wheel hashes are observed, not proof of new Stage-B packaging or release acceptance.
- production: no BUILD_PASS, LOCAL_HOST_QUALIFIED, PORTAL_APPROVED, PUBLISHED, PRODUCTION_VERIFIED, PIPD_S4_FULL_PASS, deployment or production authorization. Public release remains FAIL-CLOSED / HOLD.
- live/external side effect: no real model/provider calls, broker writes, remote publication, push, deployment, or product suite. No model identity can be authenticated.
- A stub proves control flow and nothing about a model; a clean git status is not code correctness; passing tests are not release acceptance.

## 11. Recommended next route

`RESUME_SMALLEST_REPAIR`

Route FCR-001/002/003/004 to gate/child admission and physical log containment; FCR-005/006 to checker failure classification and final evidence lifecycle. Scope candidate repair to these round-owned harness components and their focused external fixtures. Do not modify unrelated product/core, release assets, existing authority, or historical evidence to obtain PASS. Keep frozen commit as rollback/checkpoint. Require mixed-error, role/provider, qualify-only and junction focused cases, affected three-suite regression and fresh independent read-only recheck. Route FCR-007/009 separately to evidence owner for a new current index; preserve historical records. This challenger performs no repair, acceptance promotion or authority edit.

## 12. External inspection list

Inspection priority: findings.json; numbered-sources.txt; source-inventory.json; gate-junction.txt and junction-readback.txt; owner-regex-qualify-only-isolated.txt; effort-qualify-only-isolated.txt; other-role-owner-isolated.txt; provider-override-isolated.txt; checker-mixed-failure.txt; frozen old evidence pack and AO9 path discrepancy. Orchestration-locators.json names retained temp drivers and SHA-256. Case reproducer records include exact shell setup and invocation; replace retained private temp root with a new independently frozen external clone if replaying later, and reset only that external clone to the named commit before each case. A replay must configure executable shell stubs for CODEX_BIN/GATE_BIN; never omit them and accidentally launch a real model.

Host adjustments and failed attempts: rg was unavailable, so native file discovery was used. Initial review-relative file paths were corrected to round-relative location. Native C:/ paths given to MSYS shell mkdir caused first suite fixture failures (harness 5/33 exit 1, gate 3/31 exit 1, checker 6/8 exit 1), retained in the unsuffixed suite result files. POSIX /tmp shell paths with inherited MSYS conversion overrides removed then worked with native Git/Python argument translation. Native subprocess paths (archive, Python orchestration, clone) used C:/ or Windows native paths. Gate path-only retry still saw inherited lane settings (15/19 exit 1); those values were not evidence about candidate behavior. Final isolated runs clear only relevant LANE_/GATE_/ROUTE_/STUB_/FO_ settings and startup/path-conversion variables without dumping environment. A first forged-receipt case was stopped early by inherited model setting (exit 6), and was rerun isolated to reach actual receipt validation (exit 8); both results retained. No delivery Git configuration changed. Checker suite's hardcoded temp LOGD was rebound in a private temp copy to avoid deleting a shared temp directory; all suite copies normalize CRLF to LF. Provenance files record source/executed hashes and adaptation; candidate scripts were unchanged. The source comparison separately verifies candidate object equality after line-ending normalization.

